      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "I'm planning out the full structure: handling people with validation and dedup, expenses with split logic distributing leftover cents fairly, exposing balances via data attributes, and designing a greedy settle-up algorithm that minimizes transfers to at most n-1.\n\nI also need to decide on money formatting with Intl.NumberFormat for CAD, persist state in localStorage, and pick fonts and colors from the allowed set, plus consistent focus styling. For amount parsing I'll keep the inputs as text fields and validate with a regex that allows up to two decimals while stripping stray commas or dollar signs, and I want to preserve whatever the user typed if validation fails rather than clearing the field.\n\nSince forms stay static and only payer options or split checkboxes update dynamically, adding a person should preserve existing checkbox states while auto-checking the new one, whereas submitting an expense resets the form with everything checked again. Expense list items need description, amount, payer, and split members with a remove button, and balances should render in Kelp for positive, Harbour for negative. For settle-up I'll compute suggested payments in cents using a greedy largest-debtor-to-largest-creditor pairing for deterministic results, and I need to handle empty states—no people shows a prompt message, and adding an expense with no people should either disable the button or surface a validation error.\n\nI'm laying out the app as a single centered column of panels styled with Paper backgrounds and Rope borders. For the data model I'm using people as a name array and expenses as objects with id, description, cents, payer, and split array, displaying expenses in reverse chronological order. Share calculation divides cents evenly with the remainder distributed to the first few people by index, and balances come from paid minus share; the settle-up algorithm sorts creditors and debtors by magnitude and greedily matches them down to zero. I'm also adding accessibility touches—fieldset/legend for the split selector and an aria-live alert region for error messages.\n\nRather than toggling a hidden attribute on the error message, I'll dynamically insert or clear the error paragraph from the DOM so it's truly absent when there's no error. I'm picking Harbour as the checkbox accent color and pulling in Fraunces and IBM Plex Sans from Google Fonts for headings and body text, then writing the base CSS for background, text color, heading sizes, and responsive padding.\n\nContinuing with input and button styling...\n\nFor the expense list item I'm using a grid layout for description/amount with metadata in Fog at 14px, and tabular numbers for money values. Now I'm starting the JS, setting up a storage key constant and a currency formatter using Intl.NumberFormat for CAD.\n\nValidating state loading with try/catch, then writing render functions for people, the expense form, expenses list, balances, and transfers, preserving selected payer and checked split boxes where possible and defaulting new entries sensibly. For adding a person I'm validating the name isn't empty or duplicate, with friendly error messages like noting someone with that name is already on the trip, then saving and refocusing the input; for adding an expense I'm validating the description and amount fields similarly.\n\nI'm parsing the amount string to strip currency symbols and commas, validating it's a positive number with at most two decimal places, converting it into integer cents carefully using string splitting rather than float math to avoid rounding errors, and giving specific error messages depending on whether the amount is missing, malformed, or non-positive. I'm also checking that at least one person exists as a possible payer and at least one person is checked for the split before allowing the expense to be added, then resetting the form fields and refocusing afterward.\n\nFor deletion I filter the expense out by id, persist, and re-render. For IDs I'm using a simple incrementing counter stored in state rather than relying on timestamps. I'm building each expense list item as a DOM node using a small helper function, with safe text content to avoid injecting HTML, showing the description, amount, who paid, and who it's split between, plus a delete button labeled with the expense name for accessibility. For the balances section, each person gets a list item with their name, their net amount, and a label indicating whether they're owed money, owe money, or are settled up, and I'm starting to work out how to represent the actual transfer suggestions between people.\n\nI'm adding transfer entries showing who pays whom and how much, with data attributes identifying both people. I'm also defining empty-state messages for when there are no people yet or when everyone is already even, sentence-casing the headings, and writing the wordmark and tagline markup. I'm also deciding how to structure the header relative to the main content wrapper — likely just nesting it inside main for simplicity.\n\n",
